From b84b3c09d3399ceac113dd4cfda78829bc16beb5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: RitzDaCat <49771174+RitzDaCat@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 10:49:08 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] bpf: add native scalar bit operation kfuncs

BPF programs have no instruction for count-trailing-zeros, popcount,
count-leading-zeros or the high half of a 64x64 multiply. Schedulers
that walk CPU masks fall back to the compiler builtins, which lower to
multi-instruction sequences on every call.

Add four kfuncs, bpf_ctz64(), bpf_popcnt64(), bpf_clz64() and
bpf_mulhi64(), with exact C implementations. On x86-64 the JIT emits
TZCNT, POPCNT, LZCNT or MUL inline instead of a call when the CPU has
BMI1, POPCNT or ABM. The unary ops are KF_FASTCALL, and the verifier
bounds their return value to 0..64 so callers can index arrays without
an extra mask.

Measured on a Ryzen 7 9800X3D with scx_cake (bpf_stats ns/run, 20 s
slots): ops.running -2.31 ns, ops.select_cpu -0.2 to -4.11 ns. No
frame-time change was measurable in a CPU-limited game.

Programs that do not call the kfuncs are unaffected. A
scalar_bitops selftest is included.
